{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_vrtx_018xbokjJVU9uemyMTJyv32p","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Skill-Authoring Patterns\n2\t\n3\t14 patterns for writing effective Claude Code SKILL.md files. Organized by category with detection signals, quality criteria, and improvement guidance for each.\n4\t\n5\t## Discovery\n6\t\n7\t### 1. Activation Metadata\n8\t\n9\t**Applicability**: Universal\n10\t\n11\t**What it is**: YAML frontmatter with `name`, `description`, and optional fields (`argument-hint`, `user-invocable`, `disable-model-invocation`) that controls when Claude selects this skill.\n12\t\n13\t**Detection signals**:\n14\t- YAML frontmatter block exists (`---` delimiters)\n15\t- `name` field present with namespaced format (`plugin:skill`)\n16\t- `description` field present with action-oriented text\n17\t- Description includes trigger phrases matching intended use cases\n18\t\n19\t**Quality criteria**:\n20\t- **Strong**: Description names specific trigger verbs and exclusion phrases. A reader can predict which requests activate it without reading the body. `argument-hint` present if the skill accepts input.\n21\t- **Present**: Frontmatter exists but description is vague or generic (\"Helps with X\").\n22\t\n23\t**Improvement guidance**: Rewrite the description so it reads like a routing rule. List the 3-5 most common user phrasings that should trigger this skill. Add `argument-hint` if the skill takes input.\n24\t\n25\t---\n26\t\n27\t### 2. Exclusion Clause\n28\t\n29\t**Applicability**: Universal\n30\t\n31\t**What it is**: A \"Do NOT use for\" or \"Skip when\" statement in the description or body that prevents false activation.\n32\t\n33\t**Detection signals**:\n34\t- Explicit negation phrases in description or early body (\"Do NOT use\", \"Skip when\", \"Not for\")\n35\t- Anti-patterns or non-trigger scenarios listed\n36\t- Clear boundary between this skill and adjacent ones\n37\t\n38\t**Quality criteria**:\n39\t- **Strong**: Names specific adjacent skills or scenarios that are commonly confused with this one. Exclusions are actionable (Claude can route away based on them).\n40\t- **Present**: Generic exclusion exists (\"not for general use\") without naming what to use instead.\n41\t\n42\t**Improvement guidance**: Identify the 2-3 most likely misroutes. Name the adjacent skill or behavior Claude should choose instead. Place exclusions in the description (not buried in the body) so routing checks see them.\n43\t\n44\t---\n45\t\n46\t## Context Economy\n47\t\n48\t### 3. Context Budget\n49\t\n50\t**Applicability**: Universal\n51\t\n52\t**What it is**: Awareness of token cost. The skill loads only what it needs and avoids pulling in large reference material unconditionally.\n53\t\n54\t**Detection signals**:\n55\t- Conditional loading (\"if X exists, read it\")\n56\t- Scoped reads (specific files, line ranges, sections) rather than \"read everything\"\n57\t- No unconditional loading of large knowledge files or entire directories\n58\t- File size awareness (line limits, pagination)\n59\t\n60\t**Quality criteria**:\n61\t- **Strong**: Every read or load is conditional or scoped. Knowledge files are loaded only when the skill's logic requires them. No \"load all files in directory\" patterns.\n62\t- **Present**: Some files are loaded conditionally, but others are loaded unconditionally regardless of need.\n63\t\n64\t**Improvement guidance**: Audit every Read/Glob/Grep instruction. Make large loads conditional on whether the skill's current path needs them. Prefer targeted reads (specific sections, line ranges) over full-file reads for large files.\n65\t\n66\t---\n67\t\n68\t### 4. Progressive Disclosure\n69\t\n70\t**Applicability**: Conditional (skills longer than ~200 lines or with distinct reference material)\n71\t\n72\t**Condition**: The skill has enough content that splitting into a main SKILL.md plus reference files improves readability and reduces token cost.\n73\t\n74\t**Detection signals**:\n75\t- SKILL.md stays under 500 lines\n76\t- Reference material split into separate files in the same directory\n77\t- SKILL.md references companion files with conditional loading\n78\t- Clear separation between core instructions and supplementary material\n79\t\n80\t**Quality criteria**:\n81\t- **Strong**: Core workflow fits in SKILL.md. Reference data, examples, and edge cases live in companion files loaded only when needed. Companion files have descriptive names.\n82\t- **Present**: SKILL.md is long but has some structure, or references exist but are loaded unconditionally.\n83\t\n84\t**Improvement guidance**: Move reference tables, example libraries, and edge-case catalogs into companion files. Keep the main SKILL.md focused on the decision-making workflow. Name companion files descriptively (e.g., `examples.md`, `reference.md`, `gotchas.md`).\n85\t\n86\t---\n87\t\n88\t## Instruction Calibration\n89\t\n90\t### 5. Control Tuning\n91\t\n92\t**Applicability**: Universal\n93\t\n94\t**What it is**: The right level of specificity in instructions. Rigid steps where precision matters, flexible guidance where judgment matters.\n95\t\n96\t**Detection signals**:\n97\t- Mix of exact commands/formats (rigid) and open-ended guidance (flexible)\n98\t- Rigid sections for output formats, validation steps, error handling\n99\t- Flexible sections for analysis, creative decisions, user interaction\n100\t- No over-specification of judgment calls or under-specification of exact outputs\n101\t- No weak control-flow declarations: steps or headings labeled \"Optional\", \"if desired\", \"consider\", or \"you may\" when the step contains conditional logic that already handles the skip case (making the \"Optional\" label redundant and permission-granting)\n102\t\n103\t**Quality criteria**:\n104\t- **Strong**: A reader can tell which parts are \"follow exactly\" vs. \"use your judgment.\" Output formats are specified precisely. Analysis and reasoning steps allow adaptation. Workflow steps that have their own branching logic (e.g., \"if available, do X; if not, skip\") are not additionally labeled \"Optional\" in headings or introductory text, because the branch already encodes the skip condition. No step uses soft language (\"may\", \"consider\", \"optionally\") on actions that must happen when their condition is met.\n105\t- **Present**: Instructions exist but everything is the same level of specificity (either all rigid or all vague). Or: steps have correct branching logic but headings or framing undermine it with soft labels that invite skipping the branch check entirely.\n106\t\n107\t**Improvement guidance**: Mark output-format sections as rigid (exact templates, required fields). Mark analysis and decision sections as flexible (criteria to consider, not exact steps to follow). Use imperative verbs for rigid steps, descriptive language for flexible ones. Audit step headings and introductory sentences for weak control-flow declarations: if a step says \"(Optional)\" but contains \"if X, do Y; if not, skip,\" remove the \"Optional\" label. The branching logic is the control flow; the label undermines it by giving the LLM permission to skip the branch check itself.\n108\t\n109\t---\n110\t\n111\t### 6. Explain-the-Why\n112\t\n113\t**Applicability**: Universal\n114\t\n115\t**What it is**: Reasoning behind non-obvious instructions so Claude can make good judgment calls in edge cases.\n116\t\n117\t**Detection signals**:\n118\t- \"Because\", \"so that\", \"this ensures\", \"the reason\" phrases near instructions\n119\t- Context for constraints (\"500 lines because context budget\")\n120\t- Rationale for ordering choices, format decisions, or workflow steps\n121\t\n122\t**Quality criteria**:\n123\t- **Strong**: Every non-obvious constraint includes its reason. A reader could infer the right action in an unlisted edge case by understanding the intent.\n124\t- **Present**: Some reasons given, but many instructions are bare imperatives without context.\n125\t\n126\t**Improvement guidance**: For each instruction, ask \"would Claude know why if this failed?\" If not, add a brief reason. Focus on constraints, ordering requirements, and format choices. One sentence is enough.\n127\t\n128\t---\n129\t\n130\t### 7. Template Scaffold\n131\t\n132\t**Applicability**: Conditional (skills that produce structured output)\n133\t\n134\t**Condition**: The skill generates output with a consistent structure (reports, checklists, code, formatted responses).\n135\t\n136\t**Detection signals**:\n137\t- Output templates with placeholders\n138\t- Markdown/code blocks showing expected output structure\n139\t- Field descriptions for template variables\n140\t- Example outputs matching the template\n141\t\n142\t**Quality criteria**:\n143\t- **Strong**: Complete output template with all fields defined. Placeholders use descriptive names. At least one filled example demonstrates the template in use.\n144\t- **Present**: Partial template or output described in prose without a concrete example.\n145\t\n146\t**Improvement guidance**: Write the full output template with placeholder names that describe expected content. Add one filled example. If the output varies by scenario, show the most common variant as the template and describe variations separately.\n147\t\n148\t---\n149\t\n150\t### 8. In-Skill Examples\n151\t\n152\t**Applicability**: Conditional (skills with non-obvious input/output mapping)\n153\t\n154\t**Condition**: The relationship between input and expected output is not immediately obvious from the instructions alone.\n155\t\n156\t**Detection signals**:\n157\t- Input/output pairs showing expected behavior\n158\t- \"Example:\" or \"For instance:\" blocks with concrete scenarios\n159\t- Before/after comparisons\n160\t- Sample invocations with expected results\n161\t\n162\t**Quality criteria**:\n163\t- **Strong**: Examples cover the common case and at least one edge case. Each example shows both input and expected output. Examples are realistic, not contrived.\n164\t- **Present**: Examples exist but only cover the happy path, or show input without expected output.\n165\t\n166\t**Improvement guidance**: Add 2-3 examples covering: the most common use case, an edge case, and (if applicable) a case where the skill should decline or redirect. Show full input/output pairs.\n167\t\n168\t---\n169\t\n170\t### 9. Known Gotchas\n171\t\n172\t**Applicability**: Conditional (skills operating in domains with common pitfalls)\n173\t\n174\t**Condition**: The skill works in an area where practitioners commonly make mistakes or where counterintuitive behavior exists.\n175\t\n176\t**Detection signals**:\n177\t- \"Warning:\", \"Note:\", \"Common mistake:\" sections\n178\t- Explicit pitfall descriptions with mitigations\n179\t- \"Do not confuse X with Y\" statements\n180\t- Edge cases called out with handling instructions\n181\t\n182\t**Quality criteria**:\n183\t- **Strong**: Gotchas are specific to this skill's domain, not generic advice. Each gotcha includes what goes wrong and how to avoid it. Gotchas are placed near the relevant instruction.\n184\t- **Present**: Generic warnings exist but lack specificity, or gotchas are collected at the end rather than inline.\n185\t\n186\t**Improvement guidance**: Interview the domain for its top 3 mistakes. Write each as: \"When X happens, you might think Y, but Z.\" Place each gotcha near the instruction where it matters, not in a separate section.\n187\t\n188\t---\n189\t\n190\t## Workflow Control\n191\t\n192\t### 10. Execution Checklist\n193\t\n194\t**Applicability**: Conditional (skills with multi-step procedures)\n195\t\n196\t**Condition**: The skill has a procedure with 3+ steps that must execute in a specific order.\n197\t\n198\t**Detection signals**:\n199\t- Numbered step sequences\n200\t- Checkpoint markers between phases\n201\t- \"Before proceeding, verify...\" statements\n202\t- Clear phase/step identification\n203\t\n204\t**Quality criteria**:\n205\t- **Strong**: Steps are numbered and ordered. Each step has a clear completion criterion. Dependencies between steps are explicit. The skill can recover or halt gracefully at any checkpoint.\n206\t- **Present**: Steps exist but ordering is implicit, or completion criteria are missing.\n207\t\n208\t**Improvement guidance**: Number every step. Add a completion check after each non-trivial step. Make dependencies explicit (\"Step 3 requires output from Step 2\"). Add halt conditions where failure should stop the workflow.\n209\t\n210\t---\n211\t\n212\t### 11. Self-Correcting Loop\n213\t\n214\t**Applicability**: Conditional (skills that produce artifacts requiring validation)\n215\t\n216\t**Condition**: The skill creates output (code, files, configurations) that can be validated programmatically.\n217\t\n218\t**Detection signals**:\n219\t- \"Run tests/checks after...\" instructions\n220\t- Retry or fix loops (\"if validation fails, adjust and retry\")\n221\t- Validation commands with expected outcomes\n222\t- Maximum retry counts or bail-out conditions\n223\t\n224\t**Quality criteria**:\n225\t- **Strong**: Validation step is mandatory (not optional). Fix loop has a maximum iteration count. Bail-out produces a clear error, not silent failure. Both success and failure paths are defined.\n226\t- **Present**: Validation mentioned but optional, or no retry limit defined.\n227\t\n228\t**Improvement guidance**: Make validation mandatory after artifact creation. Define the validation command and expected outcome. Add a retry loop with a maximum count (typically 2-3). Define what happens when retries are exhausted.\n229\t\n230\t---\n231\t\n232\t### 12. Plan-Validate-Execute\n233\t\n234\t**Applicability**: Conditional (skills that make changes to user files or external systems)\n235\t\n236\t**Condition**: The skill modifies files, creates resources, or takes actions that are hard to reverse.\n237\t\n238\t**Detection signals**:\n239\t- \"First, propose...\" or \"Present plan for approval...\" phases\n240\t- User confirmation gates before execution\n241\t- Dry-run or preview modes\n242\t- Separation between analysis/planning and execution\n243\t\n244\t**Quality criteria**:\n245\t- **Strong**: Clear separation between planning and execution phases. User must explicitly approve before changes happen. Preview shows exactly what will change. Rollback or undo is addressed.\n246\t- **Present**: Some separation exists but user approval is implicit or skippable.\n247\t\n248\t**Improvement guidance**: Split the workflow into: (1) analyze and propose changes, (2) present proposal with specifics, (3) wait for user approval, (4) execute. Never combine analysis and execution in a single step for destructive operations.\n249\t\n250\t---\n251\t\n252\t## Executable Code\n253\t\n254\t### 13. Utility Bundle\n255\t\n256\t**Applicability**: Conditional (skills that need helper scripts or programmatic logic)\n257\t\n258\t**Condition**: The skill requires computation, file processing, or operations better handled by code than LLM reasoning.\n259\t\n260\t**Detection signals**:\n261\t- `scripts/` directory with helper scripts\n262\t- Bash/Python code blocks with execution instructions\n263\t- `${CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT}` references for script paths\n264\t- Tool integration (Bash, LSP, etc.) for programmatic operations\n265\t\n266\t**Quality criteria**:\n267\t- **Strong**: Scripts handle the deterministic work (parsing, validation, data transformation). LLM handles the judgment work (analysis, recommendations, writing). Scripts are referenced by path and called with clear arguments. Error handling covers missing scripts.\n268\t- **Present**: Some code exists but it's inline in the SKILL.md rather than in scripts, or scripts exist but error handling is missing.\n269\t\n270\t**Improvement guidance**: Move deterministic logic (parsing, validation, formatting) into scripts in a `scripts/` directory. Keep judgment and analysis in the SKILL.md instructions. Reference scripts using `${CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT}/scripts/` paths. Add error handling for missing or failing scripts.\n271\t\n272\t---\n273\t\n274\t## Meta\n275\t\n276\t### 14. Autonomy Calibration\n277\t\n278\t**Applicability**: Universal\n279\t\n280\t**What it is**: Frontmatter and body instructions that tune how much independence Claude has when running this skill.\n281\t\n282\t**Detection signals**:\n283\t- `user-invocable` field in frontmatter\n284\t- `disable-model-invocation` field in frontmatter\n285\t- `allowed-tools` restrictions in frontmatter\n286\t- Explicit permission gates (\"ask user before...\", \"do not proceed without approval\")\n287\t- Tool restrictions or allowlists\n288\t\n289\t**Quality criteria**:\n290\t- **Strong**: Autonomy level matches the skill's risk profile. Destructive skills require explicit approval. Read-only analysis skills allow autonomous execution. Tool restrictions are specific (not blanket allow/deny).\n291\t- **Present**: Some autonomy controls exist but don't match the risk profile (e.g., a read-only skill requires unnecessary approval, or a destructive skill lacks gates).\n292\t\n293\t**Improvement guidance**: Assess the skill's risk: does it modify files, call external APIs, or take irreversible actions? High-risk skills need `user-invocable: true`, explicit approval gates, and possibly `allowed-tools` restrictions. Low-risk skills can allow model invocation and autonomous execution.\n294\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"5c573c5d-dc25-49e2-8295-c3dd71aef14c","uuid":"fccd1be2-d4ce-457b-8a86-7818c05319b6","timestamp":"2026-05-13T10:51:06.019Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/rhuss/Development/context-engineering/cc-skill/skill/knowledge/skill-authoring-patterns.md","content":"# Skill-Authoring Patterns\n\n14 patterns for writing effective Claude Code SKILL.md files. Organized by category with detection signals, quality criteria, and improvement guidance for each.\n\n## Discovery\n\n### 1. Activation Metadata\n\n**Applicability**: Universal\n\n**What it is**: YAML frontmatter with `name`, `description`, and optional fields (`argument-hint`, `user-invocable`, `disable-model-invocation`) that controls when Claude selects this skill.\n\n**Detection signals**:\n- YAML frontmatter block exists (`---` delimiters)\n- `name` field present with namespaced format (`plugin:skill`)\n- `description` field present with action-oriented text\n- Description includes trigger phrases matching intended use cases\n\n**Quality criteria**:\n- **Strong**: Description names specific trigger verbs and exclusion phrases. A reader can predict which requests activate it without reading the body. `argument-hint` present if the skill accepts input.\n- **Present**: Frontmatter exists but description is vague or generic (\"Helps with X\").\n\n**Improvement guidance**: Rewrite the description so it reads like a routing rule. List the 3-5 most common user phrasings that should trigger this skill. Add `argument-hint` if the skill takes input.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Exclusion Clause\n\n**Applicability**: Universal\n\n**What it is**: A \"Do NOT use for\" or \"Skip when\" statement in the description or body that prevents false activation.\n\n**Detection signals**:\n- Explicit negation phrases in description or early body (\"Do NOT use\", \"Skip when\", \"Not for\")\n- Anti-patterns or non-trigger scenarios listed\n- Clear boundary between this skill and adjacent ones\n\n**Quality criteria**:\n- **Strong**: Names specific adjacent skills or scenarios that are commonly confused with this one. Exclusions are actionable (Claude can route away based on them).\n- **Present**: Generic exclusion exists (\"not for general use\") without naming what to use instead.\n\n**Improvement guidance**: Identify the 2-3 most likely misroutes. Name the adjacent skill or behavior Claude should choose instead. Place exclusions in the description (not buried in the body) so routing checks see them.\n\n---\n\n## Context Economy\n\n### 3. Context Budget\n\n**Applicability**: Universal\n\n**What it is**: Awareness of token cost. The skill loads only what it needs and avoids pulling in large reference material unconditionally.\n\n**Detection signals**:\n- Conditional loading (\"if X exists, read it\")\n- Scoped reads (specific files, line ranges, sections) rather than \"read everything\"\n- No unconditional loading of large knowledge files or entire directories\n- File size awareness (line limits, pagination)\n\n**Quality criteria**:\n- **Strong**: Every read or load is conditional or scoped. Knowledge files are loaded only when the skill's logic requires them. No \"load all files in directory\" patterns.\n- **Present**: Some files are loaded conditionally, but others are loaded unconditionally regardless of need.\n\n**Improvement guidance**: Audit every Read/Glob/Grep instruction. Make large loads conditional on whether the skill's current path needs them. Prefer targeted reads (specific sections, line ranges) over full-file reads for large files.\n\n---\n\n### 4. Progressive Disclosure\n\n**Applicability**: Conditional (skills longer than ~200 lines or with distinct reference material)\n\n**Condition**: The skill has enough content that splitting into a main SKILL.md plus reference files improves readability and reduces token cost.\n\n**Detection signals**:\n- SKILL.md stays under 500 lines\n- Reference material split into separate files in the same directory\n- SKILL.md references companion files with conditional loading\n- Clear separation between core instructions and supplementary material\n\n**Quality criteria**:\n- **Strong**: Core workflow fits in SKILL.md. Reference data, examples, and edge cases live in companion files loaded only when needed. Companion files have descriptive names.\n- **Present**: SKILL.md is long but has some structure, or references exist but are loaded unconditionally.\n\n**Improvement guidance**: Move reference tables, example libraries, and edge-case catalogs into companion files. Keep the main SKILL.md focused on the decision-making workflow. Name companion files descriptively (e.g., `examples.md`, `reference.md`, `gotchas.md`).\n\n---\n\n## Instruction Calibration\n\n### 5. Control Tuning\n\n**Applicability**: Universal\n\n**What it is**: The right level of specificity in instructions. Rigid steps where precision matters, flexible guidance where judgment matters.\n\n**Detection signals**:\n- Mix of exact commands/formats (rigid) and open-ended guidance (flexible)\n- Rigid sections for output formats, validation steps, error handling\n- Flexible sections for analysis, creative decisions, user interaction\n- No over-specification of judgment calls or under-specification of exact outputs\n- No weak control-flow declarations: steps or headings labeled \"Optional\", \"if desired\", \"consider\", or \"you may\" when the step contains conditional logic that already handles the skip case (making the \"Optional\" label redundant and permission-granting)\n\n**Quality criteria**:\n- **Strong**: A reader can tell which parts are \"follow exactly\" vs. \"use your judgment.\" Output formats are specified precisely. Analysis and reasoning steps allow adaptation. Workflow steps that have their own branching logic (e.g., \"if available, do X; if not, skip\") are not additionally labeled \"Optional\" in headings or introductory text, because the branch already encodes the skip condition. No step uses soft language (\"may\", \"consider\", \"optionally\") on actions that must happen when their condition is met.\n- **Present**: Instructions exist but everything is the same level of specificity (either all rigid or all vague). Or: steps have correct branching logic but headings or framing undermine it with soft labels that invite skipping the branch check entirely.\n\n**Improvement guidance**: Mark output-format sections as rigid (exact templates, required fields). Mark analysis and decision sections as flexible (criteria to consider, not exact steps to follow). Use imperative verbs for rigid steps, descriptive language for flexible ones. Audit step headings and introductory sentences for weak control-flow declarations: if a step says \"(Optional)\" but contains \"if X, do Y; if not, skip,\" remove the \"Optional\" label. The branching logic is the control flow; the label undermines it by giving the LLM permission to skip the branch check itself.\n\n---\n\n### 6. Explain-the-Why\n\n**Applicability**: Universal\n\n**What it is**: Reasoning behind non-obvious instructions so Claude can make good judgment calls in edge cases.\n\n**Detection signals**:\n- \"Because\", \"so that\", \"this ensures\", \"the reason\" phrases near instructions\n- Context for constraints (\"500 lines because context budget\")\n- Rationale for ordering choices, format decisions, or workflow steps\n\n**Quality criteria**:\n- **Strong**: Every non-obvious constraint includes its reason. A reader could infer the right action in an unlisted edge case by understanding the intent.\n- **Present**: Some reasons given, but many instructions are bare imperatives without context.\n\n**Improvement guidance**: For each instruction, ask \"would Claude know why if this failed?\" If not, add a brief reason. Focus on constraints, ordering requirements, and format choices. One sentence is enough.\n\n---\n\n### 7. Template Scaffold\n\n**Applicability**: Conditional (skills that produce structured output)\n\n**Condition**: The skill generates output with a consistent structure (reports, checklists, code, formatted responses).\n\n**Detection signals**:\n- Output templates with placeholders\n- Markdown/code blocks showing expected output structure\n- Field descriptions for template variables\n- Example outputs matching the template\n\n**Quality criteria**:\n- **Strong**: Complete output template with all fields defined. Placeholders use descriptive names. At least one filled example demonstrates the template in use.\n- **Present**: Partial template or output described in prose without a concrete example.\n\n**Improvement guidance**: Write the full output template with placeholder names that describe expected content. Add one filled example. If the output varies by scenario, show the most common variant as the template and describe variations separately.\n\n---\n\n### 8. In-Skill Examples\n\n**Applicability**: Conditional (skills with non-obvious input/output mapping)\n\n**Condition**: The relationship between input and expected output is not immediately obvious from the instructions alone.\n\n**Detection signals**:\n- Input/output pairs showing expected behavior\n- \"Example:\" or \"For instance:\" blocks with concrete scenarios\n- Before/after comparisons\n- Sample invocations with expected results\n\n**Quality criteria**:\n- **Strong**: Examples cover the common case and at least one edge case. Each example shows both input and expected output. Examples are realistic, not contrived.\n- **Present**: Examples exist but only cover the happy path, or show input without expected output.\n\n**Improvement guidance**: Add 2-3 examples covering: the most common use case, an edge case, and (if applicable) a case where the skill should decline or redirect. Show full input/output pairs.\n\n---\n\n### 9. Known Gotchas\n\n**Applicability**: Conditional (skills operating in domains with common pitfalls)\n\n**Condition**: The skill works in an area where practitioners commonly make mistakes or where counterintuitive behavior exists.\n\n**Detection signals**:\n- \"Warning:\", \"Note:\", \"Common mistake:\" sections\n- Explicit pitfall descriptions with mitigations\n- \"Do not confuse X with Y\" statements\n- Edge cases called out with handling instructions\n\n**Quality criteria**:\n- **Strong**: Gotchas are specific to this skill's domain, not generic advice. Each gotcha includes what goes wrong and how to avoid it. Gotchas are placed near the relevant instruction.\n- **Present**: Generic warnings exist but lack specificity, or gotchas are collected at the end rather than inline.\n\n**Improvement guidance**: Interview the domain for its top 3 mistakes. Write each as: \"When X happens, you might think Y, but Z.\" Place each gotcha near the instruction where it matters, not in a separate section.\n\n---\n\n## Workflow Control\n\n### 10. Execution Checklist\n\n**Applicability**: Conditional (skills with multi-step procedures)\n\n**Condition**: The skill has a procedure with 3+ steps that must execute in a specific order.\n\n**Detection signals**:\n- Numbered step sequences\n- Checkpoint markers between phases\n- \"Before proceeding, verify...\" statements\n- Clear phase/step identification\n\n**Quality criteria**:\n- **Strong**: Steps are numbered and ordered. Each step has a clear completion criterion. Dependencies between steps are explicit. The skill can recover or halt gracefully at any checkpoint.\n- **Present**: Steps exist but ordering is implicit, or completion criteria are missing.\n\n**Improvement guidance**: Number every step. Add a completion check after each non-trivial step. Make dependencies explicit (\"Step 3 requires output from Step 2\"). Add halt conditions where failure should stop the workflow.\n\n---\n\n### 11. Self-Correcting Loop\n\n**Applicability**: Conditional (skills that produce artifacts requiring validation)\n\n**Condition**: The skill creates output (code, files, configurations) that can be validated programmatically.\n\n**Detection signals**:\n- \"Run tests/checks after...\" instructions\n- Retry or fix loops (\"if validation fails, adjust and retry\")\n- Validation commands with expected outcomes\n- Maximum retry counts or bail-out conditions\n\n**Quality criteria**:\n- **Strong**: Validation step is mandatory (not optional). Fix loop has a maximum iteration count. Bail-out produces a clear error, not silent failure. Both success and failure paths are defined.\n- **Present**: Validation mentioned but optional, or no retry limit defined.\n\n**Improvement guidance**: Make validation mandatory after artifact creation. Define the validation command and expected outcome. Add a retry loop with a maximum count (typically 2-3). Define what happens when retries are exhausted.\n\n---\n\n### 12. Plan-Validate-Execute\n\n**Applicability**: Conditional (skills that make changes to user files or external systems)\n\n**Condition**: The skill modifies files, creates resources, or takes actions that are hard to reverse.\n\n**Detection signals**:\n- \"First, propose...\" or \"Present plan for approval...\" phases\n- User confirmation gates before execution\n- Dry-run or preview modes\n- Separation between analysis/planning and execution\n\n**Quality criteria**:\n- **Strong**: Clear separation between planning and execution phases. User must explicitly approve before changes happen. Preview shows exactly what will change. Rollback or undo is addressed.\n- **Present**: Some separation exists but user approval is implicit or skippable.\n\n**Improvement guidance**: Split the workflow into: (1) analyze and propose changes, (2) present proposal with specifics, (3) wait for user approval, (4) execute. Never combine analysis and execution in a single step for destructive operations.\n\n---\n\n## Executable Code\n\n### 13. Utility Bundle\n\n**Applicability**: Conditional (skills that need helper scripts or programmatic logic)\n\n**Condition**: The skill requires computation, file processing, or operations better handled by code than LLM reasoning.\n\n**Detection signals**:\n- `scripts/` directory with helper scripts\n- Bash/Python code blocks with execution instructions\n- `${CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT}` references for script paths\n- Tool integration (Bash, LSP, etc.) for programmatic operations\n\n**Quality criteria**:\n- **Strong**: Scripts handle the deterministic work (parsing, validation, data transformation). LLM handles the judgment work (analysis, recommendations, writing). Scripts are referenced by path and called with clear arguments. Error handling covers missing scripts.\n- **Present**: Some code exists but it's inline in the SKILL.md rather than in scripts, or scripts exist but error handling is missing.\n\n**Improvement guidance**: Move deterministic logic (parsing, validation, formatting) into scripts in a `scripts/` directory. Keep judgment and analysis in the SKILL.md instructions. Reference scripts using `${CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT}/scripts/` paths. Add error handling for missing or failing scripts.\n\n---\n\n## Meta\n\n### 14. Autonomy Calibration\n\n**Applicability**: Universal\n\n**What it is**: Frontmatter and body instructions that tune how much independence Claude has when running this skill.\n\n**Detection signals**:\n- `user-invocable` field in frontmatter\n- `disable-model-invocation` field in frontmatter\n- `allowed-tools` restrictions in frontmatter\n- Explicit permission gates (\"ask user before...\", \"do not proceed without approval\")\n- Tool restrictions or allowlists\n\n**Quality criteria**:\n- **Strong**: Autonomy level matches the skill's risk profile. Destructive skills require explicit approval. Read-only analysis skills allow autonomous execution. Tool restrictions are specific (not blanket allow/deny).\n- **Present**: Some autonomy controls exist but don't match the risk profile (e.g., a read-only skill requires unnecessary approval, or a destructive skill lacks gates).\n\n**Improvement guidance**: Assess the skill's risk: does it modify files, call external APIs, or take irreversible actions? High-risk skills need `user-invocable: true`, explicit approval gates, and possibly `allowed-tools` restrictions. Low-risk skills can allow model invocation and autonomous execution.\n","numLines":294,"startLine":1,"totalLines":294}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_vrtx_01Ag7dDm7nL4KtXyrxJqJ92X","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tname: \"config:validate\"\n3\tdescription: \"Validate configuration files against a schema. Use when checking config file correctness or verifying required fields.\"\n4\targument-hint: \"<config-path> [--schema <schema-path>]\"\n5\tuser-invocable: true\n6\tallowed-tools:\n7\t  - Read\n8\t  - Bash\n9\t  - Glob\n10\t---\n11\t\n12\t# Config Validator\n13\t\n14\tValidate a configuration file (YAML or JSON) against its schema. Reports missing fields, type mismatches, and constraint violations.\n15\t\n16\t## Procedure\n17\t\n18\t### Step 1: Resolve Inputs\n19\t\n20\tParse the user's input for:\n21\t- **config-path** (required): Path to the config file\n22\t- **schema-path** (optional): Path to the schema file. If not provided, look for a file named `schema.yaml` or `schema.json` in the same directory as the config file.\n23\t\n24\tIf the config file does not exist, report an error and stop.\n25\tIf no schema can be found, report an error and stop.\n26\t\n27\t### Step 2: Load and Parse\n28\t\n29\tRead both files using the Read tool.\n30\t\n31\tParse the config file as YAML or JSON based on extension. If parsing fails, report the syntax error with line number.\n32\t\n33\tParse the schema file the same way.\n34\t\n35\t### Step 3: Validate\n36\t\n37\tCompare the config against the schema:\n38\t- Check all required fields are present\n39\t- Check field types match (string, number, boolean, array, object)\n40\t- Check enum constraints (field value must be one of the allowed values)\n41\t- Check nested objects recursively\n42\t\n43\t### Step 4: Report\n44\t\n45\tFormat the results:\n46\t\n47\t```\n48\t## Validation Report\n49\t\n50\t**Config**: `<path>`\n51\t**Schema**: `<path>`\n52\t**Status**: PASS | FAIL\n53\t\n54\t### Issues Found\n55\t| Field | Issue | Expected | Got |\n56\t|-------|-------|----------|-----|\n57\t| <field.path> | missing | required | - |\n58\t| <field.path> | type mismatch | string | number |\n59\t```\n60\t\n61\tIf no issues found, report PASS with the field count.\n62\t\n63\t## Error Handling\n64\t\n65\t| Condition | Response |\n66\t|-----------|----------|\n67\t| Config file not found | Show error |\n68\t| Schema file not found | Show error |\n69\t| Parse error | Show error with line number |\n70\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"5c573c5d-dc25-49e2-8295-c3dd71aef14c","uuid":"1eb28c85-884b-4e67-a528-c9dcaee5f3e6","timestamp":"2026-05-13T10:51:06.027Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/private/var/folders/3v/16m6pn090hl4d60grm1057ww0000gn/T/agent-eval/enhance-opt-iter-1/cases/case-002-present-to-strong/target-skill/SKILL.md","content":"---\nname: \"config:validate\"\ndescription: \"Validate configuration files against a schema. Use when checking config file correctness or verifying required fields.\"\nargument-hint: \"<config-path> [--schema <schema-path>]\"\nuser-invocable: true\nallowed-tools:\n  - Read\n  - Bash\n  - Glob\n---\n\n# Config Validator\n\nValidate a configuration file (YAML or JSON) against its schema. Reports missing fields, type mismatches, and constraint violations.\n\n## Procedure\n\n### Step 1: Resolve Inputs\n\nParse the user's input for:\n- **config-path** (required): Path to the config file\n- **schema-path** (optional): Path to the schema file. If not provided, look for a file named `schema.yaml` or `schema.json` in the same directory as the config file.\n\nIf the config file does not exist, report an error and stop.\nIf no schema can be found, report an error and stop.\n\n### Step 2: Load and Parse\n\nRead both files using the Read tool.\n\nParse the config file as YAML or JSON based on extension. If parsing fails, report the syntax error with line number.\n\nParse the schema file the same way.\n\n### Step 3: Validate\n\nCompare the config against the schema:\n- Check all required fields are present\n- Check field types match (string, number, boolean, array, object)\n- Check enum constraints (field value must be one of the allowed values)\n- Check nested objects recursively\n\n### Step 4: Report\n\nFormat the results:\n\n```\n## Validation Report\n\n**Config**: `<path>`\n**Schema**: `<path>`\n**Status**: PASS | FAIL\n\n### Issues Found\n| Field | Issue | Expected | Got |\n|-------|-------|----------|-----|\n| <field.path> | missing | required | - |\n| <field.path> | type mismatch | string | number |\n```\n\nIf no issues found, report PASS with the field count.\n\n## Error Handling\n\n| Condition | Response |\n|-----------|----------|\n| Config file not found | Show error |\n| Schema file not found | Show error |\n| Parse error | Show error with line number |\n","numLines":70,"startLine":1,"totalLines":70}}}
{"type": "assistant", "message": {"model": "claude-opus-4-6", "id": "msg_vrtx_015bh7XcoGqzezd9FQWqqCWG", "type": "message", "role": "assistant", "content": [{"type": "thinking", "thinking": "Good, I have both files. Let me proceed through the steps.\n\n**Step 1: Input Handling** - File exists, has frontmatter with name and description. \u2713\n\n**Step 2: Load Knowledge** - Loaded. \u2713\n\n**Step 3: Baseline Evaluation**\n\nLet me evaluate each of the 14 patterns:\n\n1. **Activation Metadata**: The frontmatter has name, description, argument-hint, user-invocable, allowed-tools. The description says \"Validate configuration files against a schema. Use when checking config file correctness or verifying required fields.\" It has trigger phrases. It has argument-hint. \n\nThe description gives some specific trigger language around config validation and field verification, which is decent, but it lacks multiple trigger verb phrasings and exclusion phrases that would make it stronger. I'd mark this as present but not fully developed.\n\n2. **Exclusion Clause**: There's no explicit exclusion clause anywhere\u2014no \"Do NOT use\" or \"Skip\" guidance.\n\n3. **Context Budget**: The skill reads both config and schema files as needed, which is purposeful and avoids unnecessary loading. However, there's no explicit mention of handling large files or conditional loading strategies, so the context awareness is present but not optimized.\n\n4. **Progressive Disclosure**: The skill is around 70 lines, well under the 200-line threshold, so this criterion doesn't apply.\n\n5. **Control Tuning**: The procedure has structured steps with some rigid elements, particularly in the output template and initial checks, but there's no clear separation between what's flexible and what's fixed.\n\n6. **Explain-the-Why**: The instructions are mostly bare imperatives without reasoning\u2014there's no explanation for why certain parsing approaches are chosen or why recursive checking is necessary.\n\n7. **Template Scaffold**: A validation report template exists in the output section with placeholder fields, but there's no filled example showing what the output actually looks like.\n\n8. **In-Skill Examples**: The transformation from config and schema inputs to a validation report isn't obvious, and there are no concrete input/output examples to clarify the process.\n\n9. **Known Gotchas**: Looking at potential edge cases and common pitfalls...\n\n10. **Execution Checklist**: The skill has four numbered steps with an implicit ordering, but the completion criteria for each step aren't always clear, and dependencies between steps are left implicit rather than explicit.\n\n11. **Self-Correcting Loop**: The skill itself is a validator that produces a validation report as output. I'm wondering whether the report itself could be incorrect and need validation, or if the skill's output is final as-is.\n\n12. **Plan-Validate-Execute**: The skill is read-only\u2014it uses tools to read and report on configurations without modifying any external systems.\n\n13. **Utility Bundle**: The validation logic could potentially benefit from helper scripts, but for straightforward schema checks, the LLM reasoning is sufficient without them.\n\n14. **Autonomy Calibration**: The skill has appropriate autonomy settings with `user-invocable: true` and specific tool restrictions (Read, Bash, Glob), which aligns well with its read-only nature and low-risk profile.\n\nNow stepping back to evaluate the overall pattern: activation metadata is present but could be more detailed with additional trigger phrasings and exclusion phrases, there's no exclusion clause, context handling exists but doesn't explicitly address large files, progressive disclosure doesn't apply given the skill's size, control tuning is present though the instruction specificity is consistent throughout, and the explain-the-why rationale is missing.\n\nLooking at the remaining patterns: templates exist without filled examples, there are no in-skill examples or documented gotchas despite config validation having known pitfalls, the execution checklist has numbered steps but incomplete completion criteria, and self-correcting loops and plan-validate-execute patterns don't apply to a read-only skill. The utility bundle pattern also doesn't apply since the skill is straightforward enough for LLM reasoning. The autonomy calibration is strong and appropriate for this read-only use case, leaving patterns 1, 2, 3, and 5 as the most applicable gaps to address. Writing the enhanced skill... If parsing fails, I'll report the syntax error with its line number. The schema gets parsed the same way, though YAML has some quirks\u2014anchors and aliases can silently duplicate values, and unquoted strings like `yes` or `no` become booleans, which might look like type mismatches at first glance.\n\nThen I'm comparing the config against the schema by checking that all required fields exist, verifying field types match, validating enum constraints, and recursively validating nested objects at every level. A key thing to watch for is nested required fields\u2014a `database` block might exist but be missing its required `host` sub-field.\n\nWhen I report type mismatches, I'll show both the expected type from the schema and the actual value from the config so the user can see whether the value itself is wrong or if the schema needs adjusting. The results go into a formatted report with a table showing each field, the issue, what was expected, and what was actually found\u2014or just a PASS status if validation succeeds.\n\nGenerating markdown table... I'm evaluating whether we have enough examples to meet the \"strong\" criteria\u2014we've got one concrete failing config scenario, which covers the common case, but we'd ideally want an edge case example too to fully satisfy that requirement. it **strong** for Template Scaffold since the example demonstrates the template in action, but **present** for In-Skill Examples because it only shows output without the corresponding input. I've also added YAML type coercion and nested validation gotchas inline near the relevant sections, which brings that pattern to **strong**. Now I'm working through the Execution Checklist, numbering the steps and defining where to halt...\n\nLooking at the overall improvements, I've moved 8 patterns up in strength\u2014Activation Metadata, Exclusion Clause, Context Budget, Control Tuning, Explain-the-Why, Template Scaffold, Execution Checklist all got stronger, and In-Skill Examples improved from absent to present. Autonomy Calibration stayed strong. That's solid progress. Let me now examine Step 7 more closely.", "signature": ""}], "stop_reason": null, "stop_sequence": null, "stop_details": null, "usage": {"input_tokens": 4210, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 1023, "cache_read_input_tokens": 37697, "cache_creation": {"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens": 1023, "ephemeral_1h_input_tokens": 0}, "output_tokens": 8}, "context_management": null}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "5c573c5d-dc25-49e2-8295-c3dd71aef14c", "uuid": "e615b741-4f21-4ba3-a6f9-7802f0cfa54e", "timestamp": "2026-05-13T10:52:33.711Z"}
{"type": "assistant", "message": {"model": "claude-opus-4-6", "id": "msg_vrtx_015bh7XcoGqzezd9FQWqqCWG", "type": "message", "role": "assistant", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "### Steps Executed\n- Step 1: Input Handling, completed\n- Step 2: Load Knowledge, completed\n- Step 3: Baseline Evaluation, completed\n- Step 4: Already-Optimal Detection, not optimal, continued\n- Step 5: Enhancement Targeting, 9 targets selected\n- Step 6: Rewrite, completed\n- Step 7: File Splitting, skipped (under 500 lines)\n- Step 8: Prompt Plugin Delegation, skipped (plugin not available)\n- Step 9: Changelog, in progress\n\n## Enhanced Skill\n\n```markdown\n---\nname: \"config:validate\"\ndescription: \"Validate configuration files against a schema. Use when checking YAML/JSON config correctness, verifying required fields, detecting type mismatches, or auditing config files before deployment. Do NOT use for generating configs, converting between formats, or schema creation.\"\nargument-hint: \"<config-path> [--schema <schema-path>]\"\nuser-invocable: true\nallowed-tools:\n  - Read\n  - Bash\n  - Glob\n---\n\n# Config Validator\n\nValidate a configuration file (YAML or JSON) against its schema. Reports missing fields, type mismatches, and constraint violations.\n\n## Procedure\n\n### Step 1: Resolve Inputs\n\nParse the user's input for:\n- **config-path** (required): Path to the config file\n- **schema-path** (optional): Path to the schema file. If not provided, look for a file named `schema.yaml` or `schema.json` in the same directory as the config file, so that co-located schemas work without extra arguments.\n\nIf the config file does not exist, report an error and stop.\nIf no schema can be found, report an error and stop.\n\n### Step 2: Load and Parse\n\nRead both files using the Read tool. For large config files (over 500 lines), use line-range reads to avoid loading the entire file at once, as deeply nested structures can consume significant context.\n\nParse the config file as YAML or JSON based on its file extension. If parsing fails, report the syntax error with line number and stop.\n\nParse the schema file the same way.\n\nYAML files that parse without error can still have subtle issues: anchors and aliases may silently duplicate values, and unquoted strings like `yes`, `no`, `on`, `off` are parsed as booleans rather than strings. If a type mismatch looks unexpected, check whether YAML implicit type coercion is the cause before reporting it as a user error.\n\n### Step 3: Validate\n\nCompare the config against the schema. Check in this order, because later checks depend on earlier ones passing:\n\n1. Check all required fields are present\n2. Check field types match (string, number, boolean, array, object)\n3. Check enum constraints (field value must be one of the allowed values)\n4. Check nested objects recursively, validating at every nesting level\n\nA common mistake is checking only top-level keys while ignoring nested required fields. A `database` block might exist but lack its required `host` sub-field. Walk the full tree, not just the root.\n\nWhen reporting type mismatches, show both the expected type from the schema and the actual value from the config (not just its inferred type). This helps the user distinguish between a wrong value and a schema that needs updating.\n\n### Step 4: Report\n\nFormat the results using this exact template:\n\n```\n## Validation Report\n\n**Config**: `<path>`\n**Schema**: `<path>`\n**Status**: PASS | FAIL\n\n### Issues Found\n| Field | Issue | Expected | Got |\n|-------|-------|----------|-----|\n| <field.path> | missing | required | - |\n| <field.path> | type mismatch | string | number |\n```\n\nIf no issues are found, report PASS with the total number of fields validated.\n\n**Example** (failing config with three different issue types):\n\n```\n## Validation Report\n\n**Config**: `app/config.yaml`\n**Schema**: `app/schema.yaml`\n**Status**: FAIL\n\n### Issues Found\n| Field | Issue | Expected | Got |\n|-------|-------|----------|-----|\n| database.port | type mismatch | number | \"3306\" (string) |\n| logging.level | invalid enum | debug, info, warn, error | verbose |\n| cache.ttl | missing | required | - |\n```\n\n## Error Handling\n\n| Condition | Response |\n|-----------|----------|\n| Config file not found | \"Error: Config file not found at `<path>`.\" Stop. |\n| Schema file not found | \"Error: No schema found. Provide one with `--schema <path>` or place `schema.yaml`/`schema.json` alongside the config.\" Stop. |\n| Parse error | \"Error: Failed to parse `<path>` at line N: `<details>`.\" Stop. |\n```\n\n---\n\n## What Changed\n\n| Pattern Applied | What was added/changed | Why |\n|----------------|----------------------|-----|\n| Activation Metadata | Added specific trigger phrases (\"detecting type mismatches\", \"auditing config files before deployment\") and exclusion phrases to the description | Original description was functional but didn't list enough trigger scenarios for reliable routing |\n| Exclusion Clause | Added \"Do NOT use for generating configs, converting between formats, or schema creation\" to description | No exclusion existed; these are the most likely misroutes for a config-related skill |\n| Context Budget | Added guidance to use line-range reads for config files over 500 lines | Original had no handling for large files, risking context overflow on big configs |\n| Control Tuning | Made Step 3 validation order explicit (\"check in this order, because later checks depend on earlier ones passing\") and tightened error handling responses to exact message formats | Original steps were all the same level of specificity; now output format is rigid while analysis allows judgment |\n| Explain-the-Why | Added reasoning for schema co-location default, validation ordering, and showing actual values in type mismatches | Original instructions were bare imperatives without context for edge-case judgment |\n| Template Scaffold | Added a filled example showing a failing validation report with three different issue types | Original had the template but no filled example demonstrating it in use |\n| In-Skill Examples | The filled validation report example in Step 4 shows realistic output with field paths, issue types, and values | No examples existed; the mapping from config+schema to report was left to inference |\n| Known Gotchas | Added YAML implicit type coercion warning (inline in Step 2) and nested-field validation pitfall (inline in Step 3) | Config validation has well-known pitfalls around YAML booleans and shallow-only checking; neither was mentioned |\n| Execution Checklist | Added explicit halt conditions (\"Stop\") to error messages and made Step 3 ordering dependency explicit | Steps existed but lacked completion/halt criteria and inter-step dependencies |\n\n## Status Change\n\n| Pattern | Before | After |\n|---------|--------|-------|\n| Activation Metadata | present | strong |\n| Exclusion Clause | absent | strong |\n| Context Budget | present | strong |\n| Control Tuning | present | strong |\n| Explain-the-Why | absent | strong |\n| Template Scaffold | present | strong |\n| In-Skill Examples | absent | present |\n| Known Gotchas | absent | strong |\n| Execution Checklist | present | strong |\n\n**Summary**: 9 patterns improved, 0 new files proposed"}], "stop_reason": null, "stop_sequence": null, "stop_details": null, "usage": {"input_tokens": 4210, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 1023, "cache_read_input_tokens": 37697, "cache_creation": {"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens": 1023, "ephemeral_1h_input_tokens": 0}, "output_tokens": 8}, "context_management": null}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "5c573c5d-dc25-49e2-8295-c3dd71aef14c", "uuid": "df58ec05-7e50-4b95-9568-28861c86b10f", "timestamp": "2026-05-13T10:52:57.628Z"}
